  "answer": "For an FOI request, the Manual describes an opportunity to argue against release, but its wording does not promise contact in every case.\n\n**Manual practice:** The Manual says third-party consultation is required under the FOI Act where a document contains business or personal information relating to a third party. More specifically, it says the trade mark applicant or their representative **may be contacted** and given an opportunity to argue that all or part of sensitive business or personal information is exempt from release. [TMM/Part61/2/2/3; TMM/Part61/3/3/3]\n\nIf the affected party contends that information is exempt, the Manual says they have the right to request internal review and/or OAIC review **before the documents are released to the FOI applicant**. [TMM/Part61/3/3/3]\n\n**Act:** Section 217A separately requires public inspection of prescribed trade mark documents after the specified publication point. The supplied subsection does not state a pre-release consultation right, so the FOI guidance should not be treated as a guarantee covering every route by which file documents become available. No FOI Act provisions were supplied; the FOI position above is reported from the Manual, not independently verified legislation. [TMA1995/s217A; TMM/Part61/2/2/3; TMM/Part61/3/3/3]"
